# =============================================================================
# NASEK — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Both applications read the same two variables;
# they are separate deployments but one database, because a campaign is the
# same campaign on either side.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Leave them blank and `npm run dev` still runs. It falls back to on-device data
# and on-screen verification codes — useful for a demo, and honest about it: the
# sign-in screen says so in a banner. What you do NOT get is real security,
# real delivery, or data shared between browsers.
#
# That fallback is DEVELOPMENT ONLY, and enforced in two places rather than
# trusted:
#
#   * It is compiled out of any production build. `ALLOW_LOCAL_OTP_FALLBACK` in
#     `src/services/auth/otp.ts` is `import.meta.env.MODE !== 'production'`, so
#     a deployed bundle cannot generate a code even if it wanted to — it reports
#     that sign-in is unavailable instead.
#   * `npm run build` refuses to produce a production bundle at all when the two
#     Supabase variables are missing. See `assertBackendConfigured()` in
#     `vite.shared.ts`.
#
# Both exist because the alternative already happened: the deployed site was
# built without these variables, so it built no Supabase client, invented a
# six-digit code in each visitor's browser and printed it on the sign-in page.
# Nothing failed. Nothing warned. It simply authenticated everybody.
# =============================================================================

# Your Supabase project URL.
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=

# The anon / publishable key. NOT the service role key.
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys -> anon public
#
# This one is meant to be public: it is compiled into the JavaScript every
# visitor downloads, and it grants nothing on its own because row-level
# security decides what the person behind it may read.
#
# The service_role key is the opposite in every respect — it bypasses RLS
# entirely. It must never appear in this file, in any VITE_ variable, or
# anywhere else in this repository.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where each application is deployed.
#
# These are what a sign-in email points at. Leave them blank and NASEK falls
# back to the origin the page is running on, which is right in development and
# wrong everywhere else: a link to `http://localhost:5173/` is unfollowable from
# an inbox, and a preview deployment's origin changes on every push, so it is
# never on the project's redirect allow-list.
#
# Set them to the deployed address, with the scheme and a trailing slash:
#
#     VITE_SITE_URL=https://nasek.example.com/
#
# Read at BUILD time, like the two above — set them in your host's build
# environment, not at runtime.
#
# Whatever you put here must also appear in the Supabase project's
# Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs, or Supabase silently
# substitutes the Site URL and the person lands on the wrong application. Run
#
#     npm run auth:urls
#
# to check that from the outside, and `npm run auth:urls -- --apply` to set it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NASEK is three applications on three hosts. Each emails links that have to
# come back to itself, and each reads only its own variable.

# The customer website.
VITE_SITE_URL=

# The Campaign Owner Portal. This is where an invitation link lands, so getting
# it wrong sends a new owner to an application that will not let them in.
VITE_OWNER_URL=

# The administration dashboard.
VITE_ADMIN_URL=


# =============================================================================
# ADMINISTRATION, AND EVERY OTHER SECRET
#
# Nothing below goes in this file, and that is the point of this section.
#
# Vite inlines every `VITE_` variable into the JavaScript each visitor
# downloads. There is no such thing as a secret `VITE_` variable — only a
# secret that has not been read out of the bundle yet. `VITE_ADMIN_EMAIL` used
# to live here and is gone: the dashboard no longer signs in with a password
# against a named account, so there is nothing for it to name.
#
# The administration dashboard now opens on ONE ACCESS CODE, checked by the
# `admin-access` Edge Function. The code, the service role key and the SMTP
# credentials are all secrets of Supabase Edge Functions and are set with the
# CLI, never in a file in this repository:
#
#   supabase functions deploy admin-access admin-create-owner send-emails
#   supabase secrets set ADMIN_ACCESS_CODE="a long random phrase"
#   supabase secrets set NASEK_ADMIN_EMAIL="ops@nasek.om"      # optional
#   supabase secrets set RESEND_API_KEY="re_..."               # optional
#   supabase secrets set EMAIL_FROM="NASEK <no-reply@your-domain>"
#   supabase secrets set NASEK_OWNER_PORTAL_URL="https://<your owner portal>/"
#
# `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` and `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` are
# injected into every Edge Function by the platform; do not set them yourself.
#
# See docs/SUPABASE.md for the full sequence, and README.md for what each
# secret does.
# =============================================================================
